micro_lesson.content:
When analyzing time complexity, common errors include nested loops that appear linear but are actually quadratic, and using inefficient data structures like list.index() inside a loop. Consider this buggy function that searches for duplicates. The intent is to return True if any duplicate exists, but the current implementation has a subtle performance issue and a logical flaw.

```python
def has_duplicate(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False
```

Although this code is logically correct for small inputs, it runs in O(n^2) time. A more efficient approach uses a hash set to achieve O(n) average time. The error here is not a crash, but a performance anti-pattern that becomes severe as input size grows. Always profile before optimizing, and watch for redundant comparisons.

