items.solution_code:
def max_subarray_sum(arr):
    if not arr:
        return 0
    max_sum = arr[0]
    current_sum = arr[0]
    for num in arr[1:]:
        current_sum = max(num, current_sum + num)
        max_sum = max(max_sum, current_sum)
    return max_sum

micro_lesson.content:
Profiling reveals actual runtime, but theoretical complexity tells us scalability. A common mistake is assuming that a fast-running function for small n will remain fast as n grows. Consider this recursive function that computes Fibonacci numbers.

```python
def fib(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
```

This function has exponential time complexity O(2^n) because it recomputes the same subproblems repeatedly. For n = 35, it takes seconds; for n = 40, it takes minutes. Profiling with cProfile or timeit helps identify such bottlenecks. The fix is memoization or an iterative approach, reducing complexity to O(n). Always measure on realistic input sizes, not just toy examples.

